description:
pkix-path/src/lib.rs:2130–2138

The code:

    let has_leaf_any = tree.iter().any(|nd| {
        nd.depth == leaf_depth && nd.valid_policy == OID_ANY_POLICY
    });
    if has_leaf_any {
        let ap_qual = tree
            .iter()
            .find(|nd| nd.depth == leaf_depth && nd.valid_policy == OID_ANY_POLICY)
            .map(|nd| nd.qualifier_set.clone())
            .unwrap_or_default();

The tree is walked twice with the same predicate: once with .any() to check existence, then with .find() to get the node.  This is idiomatic in cases where the predicate is expensive or the data is not an iterator — but here it is a simple flat Vec scan.

Collapse into one pass:

    let ap_qual = tree
        .iter()
        .find(|nd| nd.depth == leaf_depth && nd.valid_policy == OID_ANY_POLICY)
        .map(|nd| nd.qualifier_set.clone())
        .unwrap_or_default();
    let has_leaf_any = !ap_qual.is_empty() || tree.iter().any(...);

Actually the cleanest form is:

    if let Some(ap_node) = tree.iter().find(|nd| nd.depth == leaf_depth && nd.valid_policy == OID_ANY_POLICY) {
        let ap_qual = ap_node.qualifier_set.clone();
        // ... rest of the block
    }

This eliminates the double scan and makes has_leaf_any a structural consequence of finding the node.
